 First, notice $xyz = 2 \Longrightarrow x = \frac{2}{yz}.$ Substitute into $x + y + z = 0,$ so
\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{yz} + y + z = 0
\end{align*}
Multiplying both sides by $yz,$ we get
\begin{align*}
2 + y^2z + yz^2 = 0
\end{align*}
Therefor, $y^2z + yz^2 = -2.$
Consider $(y^2z)(yz) = 2.$ We now have the system of equations
\begin{align*}
y^2z + yz^2 &= -2 \\
(y^2z)(yz) &= 2
\end{align*}
Applying the theory of symmetric polynomials to this system, we know there is a $t$ such tht $y^2z + yz^2 = t,$ then $y^2z = t - z,$ and ask how to find $z.$
Let $h(z) = t - tz -2.$ The condition that $h(t) = 0$ gives $t$.
\begin{align*}
h(t) = t - t(-2) - 2 = 0
\end{align*}
Thus, $t = -2.$ All is left is to find $y^2z = -2 - z,$ i.e., $z^2 + 3 = 0.$
\end{align*} <answer> $3$ </answer>

